{
  "esm": "1.2.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "expand-sourcetype-runspec",
    "description": "PLAN.md §7.5 step 7d, the SOURCE TYPE dimension at width five: the `expand-sourcetype` run computed from the DEFAULT DATABASE through `moves-onroad.esm`. Source types 11 (motorcycle), 21 (passenger car), 31 (passenger truck), 51 (refuse truck) and 62 (combination long-haul truck) -- two light-duty, one heavy-duty vocational, one long-haul and a motorcycle, which is as far apart as this database's physics gets.\n\nWHAT IT DISCRIMINATES over `expand-fueltype-diesel`, which is the same axis at width two.\n\n* FORTY DRIVE CYCLES RATHER THAN FOUR, and forty that disagree. A drive cycle is per VEHICLE, and a vehicle is a row of `sourceusetypephysics` -- (sourceTypeID, regClassID, model-year range), with its own rolling, rotating and drag terms, its own mass and its own fixed mass factor. These five source types select 40 of the table's 178 rows: 1 each for the motorcycle and the passenger car, 3 for the passenger truck, 15 for the refuse truck and 20 for the combination long-haul truck. So the vehicle-specific power of a drive-schedule second, the operating mode it bins into and the mode distribution the rates are weighted by differ 40 ways, and `n_physicsRow` is 40 where `expand-fueltype-diesel` needs 4 and every run in `runspecs/` before them needed 1. A motorcycle and a combination long-haul truck do not share an operating-mode distribution, and neither do a refuse truck's fifteen regulatory-class-and-model-year bands.\n\n* A COHORT COUNT THAT IS A SUM OVER FIVE UNEQUAL TERMS. 430 rows: 41 + 125 + 125 + 89 + 50. Source type 11 carries one fuel and 41 model years; 21 and 31 carry four fuels each and reach the full 125; 51 and 62 carry fuels the database has fewer sample vehicles for. 656 candidates in the grid, 430 survivors, and no declaration anywhere says 430 -- `cohortSurvivorCount` recomputes it and `tools/check-runspec-shape.py` holds the emitted relation to it.\n\n* A MODEL-YEAR RATE FACTOR NO EARLIER RUN COULD REACH. `emissionRateAdjustment` is keyed on (pollutant-process, source type, REGULATORY CLASS, fuel type, model-year range), and 26 of the national table's 1131 rows carry a factor that is not 1 at a model year this corpus runs. Every one of those 26 is heavy-duty ELECTRIC running energy. So until a run selected a source type whose electricity is heavy-duty, every cohort in this tree missed the table, 1 was the right answer, and a table read by nothing passed four RunSpecs -- `runspecs/process-tirewear.esm`, `expand-day`, `expand-counties` and `expand-fueltype-diesel` are all source type 21 or 32. This one selects source type 51, whose electricity lives in regulatory class 47 and carries 0.276035. J56 in `onroad/model.esm` is the port, and `rt_emissionRateAdjustment` is where it lands.\n\nTHE TEN SELECTIONS, ONE BINDING AND THE ORACLE are everything this document carries. Against `runspecs/expand-fueltype-diesel.esm` it differs in eleven `url_template`s and the four numbers of its binding, and in nothing else.\n\nWHAT IT REPRODUCES, as of 2026-09-20. All 430 rows of `MOVESOutput`, worst cell 9.017e-06 against a 2e-05 tolerance and worst per-pollutant emissionQuant sum 1.074e-07, in 91 seconds and 0.89 GB. It was in `runspecs/pending/` for four days and for FOUR distinct reasons, every one of them now measured rather than waited on: the source bin (PLAN.md §7.3.6), the physics row (§7.3.7), a memory ceiling that was upstream and is now fixed -- EarthSciAST #420 and #423, merged 2026-09-19, which took this run from 32 GB and killed to 0.89 GB (§7.3.8) -- and then three cells of 430.\n\nTHOSE THREE CELLS ARE WORTH THE PARAGRAPH, because three explanations were measured and rejected before the right one. Source type 51, fuel type 9, model years 2017, 2018 and 2019 came out high by exactly 3.622713 in every one of them. It was NOT the physics-row match, because no (sourceTypeID, regClassID, modelYearID) triple in `sourceusetypephysics` is covered by more than one row anywhere in the table and `run_rateRowsWithoutPhysics` is 0; NOT the electric-vehicle efficiency, because `evefficiency`'s battery and charging factors are identical across all four of this source type's regulatory classes; and NOT a bin selection, because the cohort carries exactly ONE bin with a non-zero `stmyFraction` -- regClass 47, engTech 30 -- of the 17 the model year holds, with `run_sourceBinsDropped` 0. 3.622713 is 1/0.276035, and 0.276035 is one row of one table: `emissionRateAdjustment` 9101 / 51 / 47 / 9 / 1950-2020. A constant ratio on one (source type, fuel type) pair was read as 7d's own signature -- a key plural in the data and singular in the model -- for a day, and it was a whole table the model did not read.\n\nTHE SHAPE IS RECOMPUTED AND NOT DECLARED, which is the other half of what this document holds the chain to. `n_outputCohort` is declared in the binding and `cohortSurvivorCount` recomputes it from the chain, and `tools/check-runspec-shape.py` asserts the two against the emitted keys. The same check covers the two WIDTHS this run needs above their defaults -- `n_sourceBin` 8 and `n_physicsRow` 40 -- through `run_sourceBinsDropped`, `run_physicsRowsDropped` and `run_rateRowsWithoutPhysics`, all three of which are zero here. Those are a different kind of check from the row count: a width one short does not lose a row, it loses a term of one, and the row count would not notice.",
    "authors": [
      "EarthSciML Authors and Contributors"
    ],
    "tags": [
      "runspec",
      "onroad",
      "default-database",
      "expand-sourcetype"
    ]
  },
